#: ../../../user/security-in-qubes/split-gpg.rst:392
#: c698cd2253f94350ae803555416e5fc8
msgid ""
"Current implementation requires importing of public keys to the vault "
"domain. This opens up an avenue to attack the gpg running in the backend "
"domain via a hypothetical bug in public key importing code. See ticket `#474 "
"<https://github.com/QubesOS/qubes-issues/issues/474>`__ for more details and "
"plans how to get around this problem, as well as the section on :ref:`using-"
"split-gpg-with-subkeys`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/security-in-qubes/split-gpg.rst:394
#: 85c755eddde84b97bac24f3ebc674f7d
msgid ""
"It doesn’t solve the problem of allowing the user to know what is to be "
"signed before the operation gets approved. Perhaps the GPG backend domain "
"could start a disposable and have the to-be-signed document displayed there? "
"To Be Determined."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/security-in-qubes/split-gpg.rst:396
#: 80e5587136a948b09367765c981b6cc5
msgid ""
"The Split GPG client will fail to sign or encrypt if the private key in the "
"GnuPG backend is protected by a passphrase. It will give an ``Inappropriate "
"ioctl for device`` error. Do not set passphrases for the private keys in the "
"GPG backend domain. Doing so won’t provide any extra security anyway, as "
"explained in the introduction and in :ref:`using-split-gpg-with-subkeys`. If "
"you are generating a new key pair, or if you have a private key that already "
"has a passphrase, you can use ``gpg2 --edit-key <key_id>`` then ``passwd`` "
"to set an empty passphrase. Note that ``pinentry`` might show an error when "
"you try to set an empty passphrase, but it will still make the change. (See "
"`this StackExchange answer <https://unix.stackexchange.com/a/379373>`__ for "
"more information.) **Note:** The error shows only if you **do not** have "
"graphical pinentry installed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/security-in-qubes/split-gpg.rst:400
#: f6eb58d3bfc74c06bec091c48534f47a
msgid ""
"In order to gain access to the ``vault`` VM, the attacker would require the "
"use of, e.g., a general Xen VM escape exploit or a :ref:`signed, compromised "
"package which is already installed in the template <user/templates/"
"templates:trusting your templates>` upon which the ``vault`` VM is based."
msgstr ""
